## 2.  Revised strategy (the one we will adopt)

Below is a compact pseudo‑code that incorporates the lessons above.  
All constants were tuned by hand on the last 20‑step traces; they can be refined further with a simple grid search if needed.

```text
# -------------------------------------------------
#  Parameters (tuned)
# -------------------------------------------------
k_theta   = 4.0      # gain on pole angle      (rad⁻¹)
k_thetad = 2.5      # gain on pole angular vel (rad⁻¹·s)
k_x      = 0.6      # gain on cart position   (m⁻¹)
k_xdot   = 0.5      # gain on cart velocity   (s·m⁻¹)

bias_low   = 0.004   # lower bound of bias zone (rad)
bias_high  = 0.020   # upper bound of bias zone (rad)
bias_gain  = 0.012   # tiny extra push (N)

dead_theta = 0.003   # dead‑zone on angle (rad)
dead_thetad= 0.006   # dead‑zone on angular vel (rad/s)

scale_alpha = 1.5    # scaling factor for large angles
scale_max   = 0.2    # cap for min(|θ|) inside scaling

# -------------------------------------------------
#  Controller (run every simulation step)
# -------------------------------------------------
INPUT:  state = [x, θ, xdot, θdot]

# 1.  Dead‑zone – do nothing when the pole is already
#     almost perfectly upright and not rotating.
if abs(θ) < dead_theta and abs(θdot) < dead_thetad:
    action = 0.0
else:
    # 2.  Linear LQR‑style raw force
    pole_term = k_theta   * θ   + k_thetad * θdot
    cart_term = k_x      * x   + k_xdot   * xdot
    raw = pole_term - cart_term

    # 3.  Angle‑dependent scaling (more aggressive when far)
    scale = 1.0 + scale_alpha * min(abs(θ), scale_max)
    raw   = scale * raw

    # 4.  Small bias to break symmetry when the pole is
    #    just a few milliradians off vertical.
    if bias_low < abs(θ) < bias_high:
        raw += bias_gain * sign(θ)

    # 5.  Clip to the admissible force range.
    if raw >  3.0: action =  3.0
