## 2. Updated strategy (the one we will adopt)

Below is a compact pseudo‑code that incorporates the lessons above.  
All constants were tuned by hand on the basis of the observed trajectories; they can be further refined with a simple grid‑search if needed.

```text
/* -------------------------------------------------------------
   CART‑POLE BALANCING CONTROLLER – version 4
   -------------------------------------------------------------
   State variables (input each step):
       x        – cart position (m)
       x_dot    – cart velocity (m/s)
       theta    – pole angle   (rad)   (0 = upright)
       theta_dot– pole angular velocity (rad/s)

   Persistent internal variable:
       drift_sum  – bounded integral term, initialised to 0
   ------------------------------------------------------------- */

IF NOT is_finite(x) OR NOT is_finite(x_dot) OR NOT is_finite(theta) OR NOT is_finite(theta_dot)
    THEN force ← 0
ELSE IF abs(theta) ≥ 0.20
    /* hard safety stop – push hard toward centre */
    THEN force ← -3.0 * sign(theta)
ELSE
    /* ---------- 1. pole linear stabiliser (PD) ---------- */
    kp      ← 4.2
    kd      ← 2.6
    pole_pd ← kp * theta + kd * theta_dot

    /* ---------- 2. angle‑dependent viscous damping ---------- */
    damp_coeff ← 0.5 + 0.5 * (abs(theta) / 0.20)      /* 0.5 … 1.0 */
    pole_damp  ← -damp_coeff * theta_dot

    /* ---------- 3. adaptive recovery boost ---------- */
    /*   The boost magnitude grows linearly with how close we are to the failure angle   */
    IF abs(theta) > 0.15 AND theta * theta_dot > 0
        THEN
            /* distance to limit (0 … 0.05) */
            dist_to_limit ← 0.20 - abs(theta)          /* 0.05 … 0.05 */
            boost_gain    ← 0.6 + 4.0 * (0.05 - dist_to_limit)   /* 0.6 … 2.6 */
            recovery      ← -boost_gain * sign(theta)
        ELSE recovery ← 0

    /* ---------- 4. cart‑centering (always‑on, very low gain) ---------- */
    /*   This term is tiny so it never fights the pole PD, but it prevents the cart
         from drifting to the track edge over long episodes. */
    cent_gain_pos  ← 0.08          /* position gain */
    cent_gain_vel  ← 0.04          /* velocity gain */
    cart_term_raw  ← cent_gain_pos * x + cent_gain_vel * x_dot
    cart_term      ← clip(cart_term_raw, -0.8, +0.8)   /* keep it small */

    /* ---------- 5. strong centering when pole is very near vertical ---------- */
    /*   When the pole is almost upright we can afford a slightly larger centering
         term without destabilising the pole. */
    IF abs(theta) < 0.08 AND abs(theta_dot) < 0.10
        THEN
            strong_center ← clip(0.25 * x + 0.15 * x_dot, -1.2, +1.2)
        ELSE strong_center ← 0

    /* ---------- 6. tiny symmetry‑breaking bias ---------- */
    IF 0.003 < abs(theta) AND abs(theta) < 0.015 AND abs(theta_dot) < 0.01
        THEN bias ← 0.004 * sign(theta)
        ELSE bias ← 0

    /* ---------- 7. integral‑like drift compensation ---------- */
    drift_sum ← clip(drift_sum + 0.0015 * sign(theta), -0.08, +0.08)

    /* ---------- 8. combine everything ---------- */
    raw ← pole_pd + pole_damp + recovery
          - cart_term          /* note the minus – we want to pull the cart toward 0 */
          - strong_center      /* strong_center is also subtracted (same direction) */
          + bias + drift_sum

    raw ← clip(raw, -4.5, +4.5)

    /* ---------- 9. smooth saturation to actuator limits ---------- */
    force ← 3.0 * tanh(raw / 3.0)      /* final force ∈ [‑3, +3] N */

END IF
```

### Why this version should beat the previous best

